        "text_hwUWJK": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Albumin Human Injection: Essential for Emergency and Critical Care",
            "text": "<h3>Medical Uses</h3><p>Albumin injections are primarily used for their ability to maintain colloid osmotic pressure in the blood:</p><ul><li><strong>Hypovolemia:</strong> Restores and maintains blood volume after trauma, surgery, or burns.</li><li><strong>Hypoalbuminemia:</strong> Corrects low albumin levels due to malnutrition, major injury, infections, pancreatitis, or liver disease.</li><li><strong>Severe Burns:</strong> Administered after the initial 24 hours to replace protein loss and stabilize cardiovascular function.</li><li><strong>Liver Cirrhosis with Ascites:</strong> Prevents fluid shifts after large-volume paracentesis.</li><li><strong>Adult Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS):</strong> Used with diuretics to manage fluid volume overload.</li><li><strong>Hemolytic Disease of the Newborn:</strong> Treats severe cases in newborns.</li><li><strong>Ovarian Hyperstimulation Syndrome (OHSS):</strong> Manages fluid shifts in severe cases.</li><li><strong>Cardiopulmonary Bypass Surgery:</strong> Used as a priming fluid.</li></ul><h3>Mechanism of Action</h3><p>Albumin maintains body colloid osmotic pressure, preventing fluid leakage from blood vessels:</p><ul><li><strong>Volume Expansion:</strong> Draws fluid from tissues into the bloodstream, increasing circulating blood volume and reducing edema.</li><li><strong>Carrier Protein:</strong> Transports hormones, enzymes, fatty acids, and drugs.</li></ul><h3>Administration</h3><p>Administered via intravenous (IV) infusion, albumin is available in 5% and 25% concentrations:</p><ul><li><strong>Dosage and Infusion:</strong> Adjusted based on the patient’s condition and response.</li><li><strong>Dilution:</strong> Should be diluted with normal saline or 5% dextrose, not sterile water.</li><li><strong>Solution Quality:</strong> Use only clear or slightly colored solutions; avoid using cloudy or particle-containing solutions.</li></ul><h3>Risks and Contraindications</h3><p>While adverse reactions are rare, careful consideration is necessary:</p><ul><li><strong>Allergic Reactions:</strong> Not suitable for those with hypersensitivity to albumin; symptoms include hives and wheezing.</li><li><strong>Fluid Overload:</strong> Risk for patients with severe heart<br/> failure, pulmonary edema, or hypertension. Monitor for overload signs <br/>like shortness of breath.</li><li><strong>Severe Anemia:</strong> Albumin does not carry oxygen and may dilute red blood cells, impacting oxygenation.</li><li><strong>Infectious Disease Risk:</strong> Extremely low risk of disease transmission due to advanced manufacturing processes.</li></ul><p>Albumin injection is a life-saving solution, essential in various medical scenarios. Always consult healthcare providers for proper administration and monitoring.</p>",
            "heading_size": "medium",
            "text_alignment": "left"
          }
        }
      },
